g\n**Subject Property:** 1000 W Crosby Rd, Carrollton, TX 75006\n**Prepared By:** Acquisitions Desk | Date: July 2026\n\n---\n\n## ⚠️ CRITICAL FLAG — READ BEFORE ANALYSIS\n\n**This is NOT a real property tax foreclosure.** The legal description on file is unambiguous: this case targets **business personal property (BPP)** — inventory, equipment, vehicles, machinery, fixtures, and trade goods belonging to Texas Granite & Tile Co. There is **no deed, no land, no structure** being foreclosed. DCAD account #99071031164000000 is a BPP account, not a real property parcel. The "$100,730 market value" reflects an assessed value of **tangible business assets**, not land or improvements. The homestead exemption notation in the intelligence data appears to be a data artifact or system crossover error — a Texas corporation cannot hold a homestead exemption, and this address is a commercial suite (Ste. 300 at 1015 N I-35 is the registered agent address, not a residential property). **PC Peak Development should not underwrite this case as a real estate acquisition.** Full analysis follows for due diligence documentation and disposition decision.\n\n---\n\n## PARAGRAPH 1 — PROPERTY & OWNER SITUATION\n\n**This is a business personal property play, not a land or structure play — full stop.** DCAD account #99071031164000000 carries a $100,730 assessed market value attributed entirely to the personal property of Texas Granite & Tile Co., a Texas corporation. There are zero improvement or land value line items because there is no real property attached to this account. The subject address — 1000 W Crosby Rd, Carrollton, TX 75006 — is almost certainly the business''s operating or former operating location in the Valwood Industrial corridor of Carrollton, a dense light-industrial and commercial district near I-35E. The Valwood Improvement Authority appearing as a taxing entity confirms the property sits within that special assessment district, which exclusively covers industrial/commerci