TUATION\n\nThis is unambiguously a **land play, not a structure play.** DCAD data signals near-zero improvement value relative to the $50,000 market value — meaning the assessed structure contributes negligible worth, and the lot itself is carrying the entire valuation. The 1/1/1900 deed transfer date is a DCAD placeholder indicating either a very old, unrecorded conveyance or an inheritance transfer that was never formally documented through a traditional deed instrument — a classic marker of **generational estate fragmentation.** The absence of a homestead exemption confirms no family member has occupied or claimed this property as a primary residence, and the payment history is brutally revealing: Levi Curl paid taxes in 1994, 1995, 1996, and 1997 — then went completely dark for approximately **28 consecutive years.** That level of disengagement suggests the original owner (Levi Curl Sr.) likely died sometime in the late 1990s or early 2000s, and the property drifted into informal family ownership with no one designating responsibility for tax obligations. The defendant roster confirms this — you have **six Curl family members** (Levi Curl Jr., Rosalind Marguerite Curl, Joan Louise Curl a/k/a Joan Elliott, Norman Wayne Curl, Ruth L. Curl, Norma Jean Curl, and Norman Wayne Curl Jr.) named as defendants, indicating the taxing authority is pursuing all potential heirs of record. This is a probate-adjacent situation where the underlying title chain is almost certainly clouded, and no single heir has taken ownership or financial responsibility. The only defendant with a confirmed address is **Norma Jean Curl** at 28657 Peach Springs Dr., Menifee, CA 92584 — she is the single most viable contact point for acquisition negotiations.\n\n---\n\n## ¶2 — FINANCIAL PICTURE\n\nThe live Dallas ACT balance sits at **$14,658.68** against a DCAD market value of $50,000, producing a **debt-to-value ratio of approximately 29.3%** — high but not yet catastrophic from a payoff standpo