play with land optionality** in the Buckner Park Addition, a post-war residential subdivision in Southeast Dallas (75217). The 1951-built, 1,168 sqft single-family residence sits on Lot 5, Block 25/6237 and carries a 45% depreciation factor per DCAD — meaning roughly **$105,489 of the $234,420 market value has already been absorbed by physical obsolescence** on a 75-year-old structure. The improvement-to-land ratio in this zip code typically skews 65/35, suggesting land value in the neighborhood of **$75,000–$82,000**, with the structure itself contributing approximately **$152,000 in assessed improvement value** — though that number must be read cautiously given the age and wear. Exterior condition data is unavailable in the pull, which is itself a yellow flag; properties in this corridor where DCAD lacks exterior notation often have deferred maintenance or limited inspector access. The physical plant is not the asset here — the **lot position, homestead tenure, and below-market entry price** are.\n\nThe ownership history tells a disciplined story: Juan and Rebeca A. Palacios acquired this property via General Warranty Deed on **July 9, 1996**, recorded in Volume 96134, Page 45 of Dallas County Deed Records — **29 years of continuous ownership** by the same household. The payment history is the most revealing data point in this file. Taxes for 2022, 2023, and 2024 were ultimately paid, but the payment sources show a household that has cycled through **mortgage servicers (EMC Mort Corp 2002, Option One Mortgage Corp 2006), a property tax lender (QBE First Property Tax Solutions LLC 2007), and most recently Lone Star Credit Union (paid 2022, 2023, 2024 alongside Juan Palacios directly)**. The 2007 QBE tax loan indicates this household has been in tax distress before and resolved it through third-party financing. The Lone Star Credit Union payments across three consecutive delinquency years strongly suggest an **active escrow or tax loan arrangement is already in plac