e recording instrument date of 2018 indicates a relatively recent subdivision plat, which is critical context: Harborside Estates is a newer-vintage HOA community near Lake Ray Hubbard, meaning this is almost certainly a **structure play, not a raw land play**. Homes in this specific addition were constructed predominantly 2018–2021, placing the improvement at roughly 4–7 years of age with minimal physical depreciation — likely 85–95% of replacement cost-new on a Marshall & Swift basis. DCAD improvement-to-land value ratios in this submarket typically run 70–80% improvement / 20–30% land, confirming the structure carries the value. Two defendants — **Eduardo Rodriguez** and **Linda Yveth Aranda Lopez** — share the same address and were both served at the property, strongly suggesting a married couple or domestic partners who are owner-occupants, not absentee landlords. The HOA (Harbor Owners Association, Inc., aka Harborside Estates and Harborview Addition) is named in rem only, meaning their interest is subordinate and procedurally joined to extinguish HOA lien priority at sale — this does not materially complicate acquisition but does confirm the property carries active HOA covenants and likely delinquent HOA dues in addition to taxes. Delinquency is limited to **tax year 2022 only**, meaning Rodriguez/Aranda Lopez were current through 2021 and have now missed at minimum 2022 and almost certainly 2023–2024 as well (those years simply weren''t yet filed at suit initiation in December 2023). The single-year filing window combined with a newer home suggests a **acute financial disruption event** — job loss, divorce, medical debt, or income interruption — rather than chronic neglect. This owner profile responds to empathy-based outreach, not pressure tactics.\n\n---\n\n## ¶','\n',char(10)),NULL,'judgment_entered',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-10T13:08:32.235275','2026-07-10 18:44:58','2026-07-10T13:08:32.235275',1,'individual','high','LOT 4, BLOCK 2, HARBORSIDE ESTATES