 **50% depreciation factor**, signaling the assessor has formally acknowledged the structure is at or near the end of its economic useful life. At $150,310 market value with a land-to-improvement ratio that — given the age and depreciation — almost certainly skews toward land value carrying the majority of assessed weight, this is **primarily a land play with a teardown or heavy-rehab overlay**, not a structure-preservation play. The improvement itself at 50% depreciation and 1,122 sq ft would pencil at roughly $60,000–$75,000 in improvement value, implying the lot carries approximately **$75,000–$90,000 of the DCAD market value** — confirming dirt value is the core acquisition thesis. The absence of a homestead exemption is a hard signal: nobody living in this house has claimed it as their primary residence, meaning it is either tenant-occupied, vacant, or managed absentee. The **deed transferred on 3/17/2026 to Little Rascals Fresh Start Inc.**, a corporate entity name that pattern-matches to a community nonprofit, small operator, or opportunistic buyer who acquired this mid-litigation — likely buying from a distressed heir or estate participant. Payment history tells a deeper story: **Lakewood Title LLC Escrow Account** has been cutting checks on taxes every single year from 2014 through 2025 — eleven consecutive years — which means a lender or servicer has been escrowing taxes, suggesting there is or was an active mortgage on this property throughout the entire litigation window. Prior payers **Lula Mae McDaniel (2014), Stricklin Mollie Mae (2000), and Frank Sally (2000)** confirm multigenerational family ownership stretching back at least 25 years. The defendant list — Stricklin, Sam Sally Jr., Ocie Sally, Frank Sally, Lula Mae McDaniel, Frances Farley, Lakeisha Cade, Sheila K. McDaniel Jones, Gloria Dean Paschal — reads as a **classic probate','\n',char(10)),NULL,'judgment_entered',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-10T13:14:10.280943','2026-07-10T13:14:10.280943','