.25}, {"entity": "Dallas County School Equalization Fund", "taxAmt": 32.11, "penaltyInterest": 18.85, "total": 50.96}, {"entity": "Dallas Independent School District", "taxAmt": 3953.41, "penaltyInterest": 2336.58, "total": 6289.99}, {"entity": "City of Dallas", "taxAmt": 2447.16, "penaltyInterest": 1441.14, "total": 3888.3}]',replace('# ACQUISITION INTELLIGENCE MEMO\n**PC Peak Development | Internal Use Only**\n**Case:** TX-23-02218 | **Court:** 191st District Court (Judge Carl Ginsberg)\n**Subject Property:** Estate of Kathryn Boyd a/k/a Kathryn Holmes Boyd\n**Analyst Date:** Current | **Distress Score:** 5/5 — HIGH PRIORITY\n\n---\n\n## ¶1 — PROPERTY & OWNER SITUATION\n\nThe subject property is a 1,164 sq ft single-family structure built in **1970**, now carrying **45% depreciation** — placing it firmly in the category of a property requiring either substantial renovation capital or tear-down consideration. At 56 years of age with no homestead exemption and no evidence of owner-occupancy, this structure has almost certainly experienced deferred maintenance compounding the actuarial depreciation DCAD has already baked in. The **$208,270 market value** as assessed breaks down in a way that warrants scrutiny: on a 1,164 sq ft house with 45% depreciation, the improvement contribution to that valuation is suppressed, suggesting the **land component is carrying significant weight** — this is likely a **hybrid land/structure play**, where the lot value in the underlying Dallas submarket may justify acquisition even if the structure is functionally obsolete. The deed transfer date of **11/11/1900** in DCAD is a data artifact indicating no clean modern conveyance has been recorded, which is a red flag for title complexity and likely reflects the property passing through informal succession rather than probated deed chains. Ownership is listed as **BOYD K EST OF 100%**, confirming no fractional interest was severed or conveyed at Kathryn Boyd''s death. Payment history tells a critical story: **Patricia C. Turner** (appearing under at least four name variants) was making tax payments through **2019 and 2020**, suggesting she was either a caretaker, occupant, or informally responsible party — possibly a family member or tenant. The estate itself made a **2019 payment**, but after 2020, all payments ceased entirely. That four-year payment gap is the distress engine driving this case.\n\n---\n\n## ¶2 — FINANCIAL PICTURE\n\nThe **live tax balance as of current is $37,614.52**, filed against a DCAD-assessed market value of **$208,270**, producing a **debt-to-value ratio of approximately 18.1%** — healthy from a lender perspective, but accelerating rapidly given Texas penalty structure. Under Texas Tax Code §33.01, delinquent taxes accrue **6% penalty in the first month**','\n',char(10)),NULL,'judgment_entered',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-10T13:16:00.249506','2026-07-10T13:16:00.249506','2026-07-10T13:16:00.249506',1,'estate','high','Being Lot 17, in Block 18/8294, Highland Hills No. 13, an Addition in the City of Dallas, Dallas County, Texas, as shown by the Warranty Deed w/Vendor''s Lien recorded in Volume 77186, Page 1287-1290 of the Deed Records of Dallas County, Texas','{"account_number": "00000801839170000", "market_value": 208270, "land_value": 70000, "improvement_value": 138270, "estimated_annual_taxes": 4637.57, "year_built": 1970, "effective_year_built": 1970, "actual_age": 56, "building_class": "13", "construction_type": "FRAME", "foundation": "SLAB", "roof_type": "", "roof_material": "", "exterior_wall": "", "living_area_sqft": 1164, "total_area_sqft": 1164, "bedrooms": 3, "bathrooms": "", "stories": "", "depreciation_pct": 45, "desirability": "GOOD", "air_condition": "CENTRAL FULL", "heating": "", "garage_sqft": null, "zoning": "SINGLE FAMILY 5K SQFT", "lot_frontage_ft": 60, "lot_depth_ft": 114, "lot_area_sqft": 6964.0, "land_unit_price": 70000.0, "legal_description": "1: HIGHLAND HILLS 13 2: BLK 18/8294 LT 17 3: 4: CO-DC 5: 8