nce in the Oaks No. 4 Addition of Garland, sitting on Lot 12, Block 28 per Instrument 202200004990 of Dallas County Deed Records. At 1,810 square feet and 49 years old with DCAD-assessed depreciation at 45%, this structure carries meaningful physical obsolescence — we''re looking at a home that''s had roughly half its useful life consumed, consistent with deferred maintenance patterns common in this vintage of Garland residential stock. DCAD pegs market value at **$283,320**, and with exterior condition data incomplete in the pull, the depreciation figure is the loudest signal we have on physical state. This is unambiguously a **structure play with land optionality** — the improvement value is carrying the majority of assessed weight at this price point in the 75043 zip, not a teardown land basis scenario. The payment history is the most analytically interesting element of this file: **Colonial Settlement Partners, LP** (a Sherman, TX-based entity served through registered agent Norton B. Jones II at 2682 White Mound Road) paid taxes in 2025, **Propel Financial Services** appears in the 2022 payment chain, and **National Title Group** and **Commonwealth Land Title Co.** appear in 2021 and 1999 respectively. This layered payment history — multiple institutional/title-adjacent entities cycling through — strongly suggests this property has been used as collateral, was subject to a tax loan (Propel Financial is a known Texas tax lender), and that Colonial Settlement Partners LP likely holds or held a lien interest that triggered their tax payment to protect position. The named defendant **Mariano Castillo** carries a homestead exemption, confirming owner-occupancy at the time of filing. **Bryen Mendez** is a co-defendant at the same address — likely a co-owner, spouse, or occupant with an interest of record. The homestead flag is critical: this is someone''s primary residence, which materially shapes both legal posture and human engagement strategy.\n\n---\n\n## 2. FINA