 / CLOSED — OWNERSHIP TRANSFERRED\n\n---\n\n## ¶1 — PROPERTY & OWNER SITUATION\n\nThis is a 1955-vintage single-family structure in the Glen Oaks Addition of Dallas County, sitting on Lot 1, Block 2/6911 of the First Section filing, title recorded under Instrument No. 201600335177. At 1,831 square feet and 71 years old with DCAD-assessed depreciation running at 45%, the physical plant is firmly in the moderate-to-heavy wear category — expect deferred maintenance on roofing, HVAC, plumbing (galvanized/cast iron era), and likely original windows and electrical panel. DCAD pegs market value at **$279,330**, but with a 45% depreciation load on improvements, the structural contribution to that valuation is carrying significant embedded obsolescence risk. This is a **hybrid play** — the lot in Glen Oaks at this price point has real underlying land value within a Dallas infill corridor, but the structure is not throwaway; a cosmetic-to-moderate rehab could preserve ARV. The exterior condition field is unpopulated in DCAD, which itself is a yellow flag — assessor either couldn''t access or didn''t update, suggesting possible limited exterior maintenance visibility. Ownership history tells an important story: **YOUNG ROM D JR** paid taxes in 2016, suggesting the property passed generationally or through estate to **William Joseph Young (50%)**, who surfaces as a defendant residing *at the subject property* (411 Town Creek Dr.) — meaning he was living in the asset during litigation. The co-ownership structure with **GARLAND JCC LLC (50%)** — a likely post-judgment or pre-arranged entity — and the **deed transfer recorded 1/9/2026** confirm this property has already moved hands. The homestead exemption was active, confirming William J. Young was owner-occupied at the subject address. The tax delinquency spanning **2019–2022** (four consecutive years of non-payment) under a homesteaded, owner-occupied structure points to financial distress, not absentee landlord neglect — this 