the improvement layer, which combined with the legal description referencing an industrial addition and the account structure, strongly suggests this is **primarily a land play** — the improvement value is negligible relative to the assessed figure, meaning the bulk of that $957,500 is sitting in dirt and location. No square footage is listed, which is consistent with a minimally improved or vacant industrial parcel. Absent homestead exemption confirms this is **non-owner-occupied**, likely held as a passive investment or business asset. The ownership chain tells a layered story: **Design Visions N** paid taxes in 2001 — likely the operating entity that originally held the property for commercial use. **Christi Byrne** then paid taxes continuously from 2020 through 2023, connecting directly to the defendant **Patrick E. Byrne** named in the suit (Gold & Silver, A Texas Joint Venture — a partnership structure suggesting this was held as a business investment between parties, not a primary residence). **Stewart Title Company** paid in 2025, which almost certainly reflects a **closing transaction** — consistent with the **deed transfer recorded 1/12/2026 into Timothy J. Morgan and Judy A. Morgan at 50/50 ownership**. Bottom line: the Byrne-affiliated joint venture has already exited. The Morgans are the current owners of record. This case is effectively **a closed chapter on the prior ownership group**.\n\n---\n\n## ¶2 — FINANCIAL PICTURE\n\nThe live DCAD tax balance on account **#26223800030120000 is $0.00** — fully cleared. The original filing amount of **$7,277.26** reflected a single delinquency year (2022), broken down as **$4,655.42 in base City of Garland tax plus $2,271.84 in penalty and interest**, with no Dallas County or DISD component visible in the filing breakdown, suggesting either separate accounts or entity-','\n',char(10)),NULL,'judgment_entered',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-10T15:19:07.508687','2026-07-10T15:19:07.508687','2026-07-10T15:19:07.508687'